{
	title: 'GREP action: portable project search that recovers from bad regexes',
	spec: 'Hal offers a GREP action behind the one native tool: GREP "pattern" { in, glob, context, files }. The pattern uses rg regex syntax. in takes a path or a list of paths (default cwd); glob limits files; context shows lines around matches; files lists matching file names only. When the pattern is not a valid regex, GREP splits it on unescaped | and searches each part as literal text, and its result says so. Missing paths do not fail the call: GREP searches the existing ones and names the missing ones in its result. Output groups matches by file as path:line: text and caps them (about 100 matches, then a count of the rest). GREP uses rg when installed and otherwise a built-in search over git-tracked and untracked non-ignored files with equivalent results for common patterns, so output and behavior match on macOS and Linux. SYSTEM.md teaches GREP for file search and says the system has no rg and not to use grep -r, whether or not rg is installed; plain grep in pipes stays allowed. Rationale: in 5000 recent BASH calls, rg regex parse errors (an unescaped ( or [ meant literally, usually inside a|b) failed about 1% of all calls on Linux and macOS; 97% of rg patterns were valid regexes, 43% plain alternation and 25% real regex, so literal-by-default would break a quarter of searches. Missing tools (rg, jq, fd) and GNU/BSD differences are avoided by not depending on the shell. Invariant: a search the model meant literally never fails on regex syntax.',
	status: 'planned',
	needs: [],
	foldInto: ['b3a']
}
